// A table is a value where a `rows` type says what its rows are: a
// field, a unique or a parameter holds one of the tables of that shape
// (or `none`, which has no rows), a fn gives one, and what holds one is
// read like the table itself. The program is closed, so the value is the
// number of the table among those of its shape that are used so, and a
// read through it asks a fn which one it is.
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/tables_as_values.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.out
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/tables_as_values.ent 2>/dev/null | cmp - %t.out
// RUN: FileCheck %s --input-file=%t.out
// RUN: ent-translate --import-ent %S/Inputs/tables_as_values.ent -I %S/../../devices \
// RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=IR

// IR-DAG: ent.enum @rows_of_x_f32_y_f32 ["none", "road_a", "road_b"]
// IR-DAG: ent.enum @rows_of_i32_by_Kind ["none", "easy", "hard"]
// IR-DAG: ent.function @row_rows_of_x_f32_y_f32__x(
// IR-DAG: ent.function @rows_rows_of_x_f32_y_f32(

// CHECK:      1: a road of 3 points, 9.0 long, ends at 4.0; a cannon costs 50
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: a road of 2 points, 8.0 long, ends at 8.0; a cannon costs 80
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: numbers 3: 1 3 5; road 2 has 0 points
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: a road of 3 points, 9.0 long, ends at 4.0; a cannon costs 80
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: a road of 2 points, 8.0 long, ends at 8.0; a cannon costs 80
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: numbers 2: 2 4 0; road 2 has 0 points
